      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Retrospective analysis with historic controls."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To analyze pin-site complications in a large series of halo thoracic braces in which regular re-tightening of the pins was performed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "Perry and Nickel first described the use of the halo thoracic brace in 1959 for cervical immobilization. Its use has been extended successfully to cervical fracture management."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A total of 266 commercially available halo thoracic braces were fitted using a standard technique. All pins were tightened routinely at 24 hours and at 1 week after application. Two data sources, prosthetic department records and patients' medical records, were analyzed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Six percent of patients had a pin-site infection; 3.7% had loose pins, and 1.1% reported pin-site pain. No subdural, intradural, or extradural abscess or cerebrospinal fluid leaks occurred. A total of 2.6% of halo rings slipped off, and 2.3% of patients experienced severe headaches."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Low rates of pin-site infection, loosening, and pain were achieved through routine re-tightening of the pins. Pin re-tightening, at 24 hours and at 1 week after application, is a safe and effective method to decrease pin-site complications."
        }
      ]},
